WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:04.380
As fun as it is to watch a great movie,&nbsp;
there’s no guarantee that you’ll walk out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:09.000
of the theater feeling good yourself. Here are a&nbsp;
few fantastic flicks where the last few minutes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:14.580
are so monumentally depressing that they’re almost&nbsp;
guaranteed to wring a few tears from the audience.

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:19.560
If you only saw the last three minutes of End of&nbsp;
Watch, you might think this cop drama was ending&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:24.780
on a happy note. The final scene features two&nbsp;
buddies having a laugh over a crazy story on what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.780 --> 00:00:29.880
looks like a pretty good day. Unfortunately,&nbsp;
context is everything. In actuality,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:34.980
the camaraderie between Officers Brian Taylor&nbsp;
and Mike Zavala is an incredibly bittersweet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:40.318
flashback scene, one that jumps back to a moment&nbsp;
before Zavala was gunned down by gangsters.

00:00:40.318 --> 00:00:44.400
Taylor and Zavala are super cops. In&nbsp;
fact, they're such good cops that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:48.726
try to take down the Sinaloa Cartel…&nbsp;
only that doesn't pan out too well.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.726 --> 00:00:53.640
After disrupting the cartel's business, the&nbsp;
officers are ambushed by heavily armed thugs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:58.020
and despite their bravery, Taylor is&nbsp;
gravely injured, and Zavala is murdered.

00:00:58.020 --> 00:01:03.900
After learning about his partner's death, Taylor&nbsp;
is absolutely shattered. Zavala was his best&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:08.280
friend in the world, and at the funeral, Taylor&nbsp;
is so grief-stricken that he can only muster&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:13.373
four powerful words that make all those happy&nbsp;
moments heartbreaking: “He was my brother.” 

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:17.820
Despite their god-like superpowers,&nbsp;
the Avengers are a sad bunch of people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:23.280
and the most tragic might just be Steve&nbsp;
Rogers, a man out of time. In Captain America:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:27.540
The First Avenger, the star-spangled&nbsp;
superhero decides to sacrifice his life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.540 --> 00:01:32.880
crashing a Hydra plane into the Arctic ice. In&nbsp;
those final moments before taking the plunge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:37.980
Cap promises to take his new love Peggy Carter&nbsp;
dancing as soon as he returns. Obviously,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.980 --> 00:01:42.205
neither expect Steve to make it back&nbsp;
home, but, well, you’ve seen the movies.

00:01:42.205 --> 00:01:46.024
“I understood that reference.”

00:01:46.024 --> 00:01:50.820
When Cap finally returns, he learns that he’s&nbsp;
been asleep for nearly seven decades. It's a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:55.740
gut punch of a moment, and the look of shock and&nbsp;
sadness on Cap's face is heartbreaking. Yeah,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.740 --> 00:01:58.980
he survived the suicide mission,&nbsp;
but the world he knew is gone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.980 --> 00:02:03.240
and he’s been separated from the woman&nbsp;
he loves for most of a century. When Fury&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:08.462
finally asks if Cap is going to be okay,&nbsp;
all his pain is summed up in one sentence:

00:02:08.462 --> 00:02:16.064
"Yeah, I just — I had a date."

00:02:16.064 --> 00:02:18.975
War movies aren't really known for their upbeat 

00:02:18.975 --> 00:02:23.280
endings, but when it comes to pure tearjerking&nbsp;
power, nothing goes after your heartstrings like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:29.940
1989’s Glory. This Civil War flick tells the story&nbsp;
of the 54th Massachusetts Infantry, which was the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.940 --> 00:02:34.860
first African-American regiment to fight for the&nbsp;
Union army. The regiment was led by Colonel Robert&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:40.020
Shaw, played in the film by Matthew Broderick, and&nbsp;
after overcoming all the racism you’d expect from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:45.570
the 1860s, Shaw and the 54th wind up leading&nbsp;
the charge in the film's climactic battle.

00:02:45.570 --> 00:02:50.820
Unfortunately, that battle doesn’t go their way.&nbsp;
After being surrounded by Confederate soldiers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.820 --> 00:02:56.580
Shaw charges the enemy, hoping to inspire his&nbsp;
men, and he's quickly gunned down. Enraged,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.580 --> 00:03:00.540
Private Silas Trip, a role that earned&nbsp;
Denzel Washington an Academy Award,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.540 --> 00:03:05.490
picks up the flag and charges after the&nbsp;
colonel, only to be shot down seconds later.

00:03:05.490 --> 00:03:10.560
Their sacrifice inspires the rest of the 54th to&nbsp;
rush out of the ravine and continue their charge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:16.020
but despite their bravery, the heroic soldiers are&nbsp;
no match for cannonballs, and all the characters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.020 --> 00:03:21.120
we've come to love — played by the likes of Morgan&nbsp;
Freeman, Cary Elwes, and Andre Braugher — are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:26.340
blown to oblivion. If that wasn't sad enough,&nbsp;
the movie ends with Shaw's and Trip's bodies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:29.386
being buried side by side in a mass grave.

00:03:29.386 --> 00:03:32.419
Gone Baby Gone isn’t exactly a feel-good film 

00:03:32.419 --> 00:03:37.620
to begin with, and the ending is appropriately&nbsp;
downbeat. Based on the novel by Dennis Lehane,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.620 --> 00:03:42.780
this thriller follows two Boston detectives&nbsp;
— Patrick Kenzie and Angie Gennaro — who are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.780 --> 00:03:47.220
hired to find a little girl named Amanda. At&nbsp;
first, they believe the kid was murdered by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.220 --> 00:03:52.650
a local drug dealer, but as they dig deeper into&nbsp;
the case, they discover a surprising conspiracy.

00:03:52.650 --> 00:03:57.420
As it turns out, Amanda's concerned uncle&nbsp;
kidnapped her from her abusive, addict mother&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.420 --> 00:04:02.700
with the help of several high-ranking cops, but&nbsp;
Patrick isn't a big believer in moral ambiguity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.700 --> 00:04:06.634
and brings brings the conspiracy down&nbsp;
and has the little girl sent back home.

00:04:06.634 --> 00:04:14.134
“You may not regret it when&nbsp;
you get home… but you will.

00:04:14.134 --> 00:04:19.320
Sure enough, it doesn’t take long for regret to&nbsp;
set in when Patrick goes to visit Amanda. He sees&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:24.780
that her mom is just as awful as ever and realizes&nbsp;
he might've damned Amanda to a life of poverty,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.780 --> 00:04:29.700
neglect, and mistreatment. In the final few&nbsp;
moments, he volunteers to babysit Amanda and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.700 --> 00:04:34.322
sits beside her, watching the traumatized,&nbsp;
lonely kid as she stares at the television. 
 &nbsp;

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:39.000
It might seem weird to say this about a&nbsp;
movie where a gorilla fights a helicopter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:43.800
but Rise of the Planet of the Apes has more&nbsp;
than its fair share of emotional moments.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.800 --> 00:04:48.780
Nothing can top the finale, when Caesar,&nbsp;
ably played by mo-cap master Andy Serkis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.780 --> 00:04:54.360
says goodbye to his human friend, Will Rodman.&nbsp;
Their relationship is the emotional crux of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:59.220
story. Caesar has known Will his entire life,&nbsp;
and the biologist is basically his adoptive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.220 --> 00:05:04.560
dad. In fact, Will is the guy who gave Caesar&nbsp;
is above-average intelligence. But after Will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:10.084
is forced to put Caesar in a primate sanctuary,&nbsp;
the chimpanzee decides it's time for a revolution.

00:05:10.084 --> 00:05:14.340
After sparking a full-blown primate rebellion&nbsp;
and a battle on the Golden Gate Bridge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.340 --> 00:05:19.800
Caesar and his army disappear into a redwood&nbsp;
forest. But before vanishing into the trees,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.800 --> 00:05:23.340
Caesar is confronted by Will, who&nbsp;
begs the chimp to come back home.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.340 --> 00:05:27.060
That's when Caesar pulls his foster&nbsp;
father close and whispers into his ear.

00:05:27.060 --> 00:05:31.724
"Caesar is home."

00:05:31.724 --> 00:05:36.227
With those three powerful words, Caesar and&nbsp;
Will realize nothing will ever be the same. 
 &nbsp;

00:05:36.780 --> 00:05:42.000
Tom Hanks movies generally leave audiences feeling&nbsp;
pretty good, but Road to Perdition is a bloody&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:47.280
exception to that cheerful rule. This gangster&nbsp;
flick finds Hanks playing Michael Sullivan Sr.,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:52.200
a mob enforcer out for revenge while trying to&nbsp;
make sure his surviving son, Michael Jr, doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.200 --> 00:05:57.600
wind up dead too. That's easier said than done&nbsp;
when you're being hunted by a psychopathic hitman.

00:05:57.600 --> 00:06:02.160
For a moment, it seems like everything&nbsp;
might be okay. Then, Michael's violent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.160 --> 00:06:07.020
past catches up with him when he's ambushed by the&nbsp;
assassin. After taking two bullets to the back,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.020 --> 00:06:11.040
Michael lies dying as the psychopathic&nbsp;
hitman assembles an old-timey camera&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.040 --> 00:06:15.600
and begins taking photos of his bloody&nbsp;
victim. Michael Jr. shows up with a gun&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:19.860
to interrupt the hitman’s sick hobby, but&nbsp;
unlike his dad, the boy isn't a killer.

00:06:19.860 --> 00:06:24.180
Fortunately, the distraction gives his father&nbsp;
enough time to gun down the assassin before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.180 --> 00:06:28.620
shuffling off this mortal coil. What really&nbsp;
seals a deal and cues the sobbing, though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.620 --> 00:06:33.698
is Michael Jr.'s final lines, answering the&nbsp;
question of whether his father was a good man:

00:06:33.698 --> 00:06:41.715
"I always give the same answer. I&nbsp;
just tell them he was my father."
 &nbsp;

00:06:41.715 --> 00:06:47.400
Directed by Martin Scorsese, Shutter Island is a&nbsp;
frightening film noir that finds Leonardo DiCaprio&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.400 --> 00:06:52.500
as Teddy Daniels, a detective who's investigating&nbsp;
a disappearance on an Alcatraz-style mental&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.500 --> 00:06:57.360
hospital. But as Teddy and his partner dig deeper&nbsp;
into the case, Teddy makes a horrible discovery:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.360 --> 00:07:03.000
he isn't really Teddy. In reality, he's&nbsp;
Andrew Laeddis, a World War II vet who killed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.000 --> 00:07:07.740
his mentally ill wife after she murdered their&nbsp;
kids. His partner is actually his psychologist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.740 --> 00:07:12.300
and the whole investigation is an elaborate&nbsp;
role-playing game meant to bring Andrew back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.300 --> 00:07:16.620
to reality. And if the game doesn't work,&nbsp;
then poor Andrew has to get lobotomized.

00:07:16.620 --> 00:07:21.540
After this shocking twist, it seems that&nbsp;
Andrew reverts back to his Teddy persona,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.540 --> 00:07:26.760
forcing his doctors to perform the lobotomy. But&nbsp;
before he's taken away to his mind-numbing fate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.760 --> 00:07:30.340
Teddy looks over at Chuck&nbsp;
and asks a haunting question:

00:07:31.140 --> 00:07:37.920
"Which would be worse? To live as a&nbsp;
monster, or to die as a good man?"

00:07:37.920 --> 00:07:42.960
It’s a simple line, but it conveys the&nbsp;
crushing sadness of Andrew’s story. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.960 --> 00:07:48.000
experiment worked. Andrew knows exactly who&nbsp;
he is and what he’s done, It’s his guilt that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:50.242
leads him to accept his grisly fate.

00:07:50.242 --> 00:07:52.200
Arnold Schwarzenegger movies aren't 

00:07:52.200 --> 00:07:57.420
generally considered tearjerkers, but the ending&nbsp;
of Terminator 2: Judgment Day has caused so many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.420 --> 00:08:02.220
sobs from macho movie fans that it’s basically&nbsp;
the action movie equivalent of The Notebook.

00:08:02.220 --> 00:08:07.620
“You think I’m unemotional! I can be emotional! I&nbsp;
cried like a child at the end of Terminator 2...”

00:08:07.620 --> 00:08:13.380
Sent from the future to protect teenage John&nbsp;
Connor, the T-800 starts out this sci-fi flick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.380 --> 00:08:17.940
as an ice cold killer robot. As he spends&nbsp;
time with the future resistance leader,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.940 --> 00:08:22.680
however, the T-800 becomes the teen's best&nbsp;
friend and father figure growing increasingly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.680 --> 00:08:26.940
lovable as he battle with a shapeshifting&nbsp;
android. He's picking up slang words,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.940 --> 00:08:30.840
developing human emotions, and learning&nbsp;
you can't just go around killing people.

00:08:30.840 --> 00:08:35.940
He also realizes that if humanity is going&nbsp;
to survive, then all Terminators need to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.940 --> 00:08:40.920
be destroyed. After defeating the T-1000,&nbsp;
Arnold realizes that if he doesn't destroy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.920 --> 00:08:46.020
the advanced AI inside his own head, that tech&nbsp;
will eventually give rise to the robot rebellion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.020 --> 00:08:51.900
that will doom mankind. So despite John's&nbsp;
pleas for him to stay, the T-800 slowly lowers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.900 --> 00:08:56.820
himself into a pit of molten steel. He gives&nbsp;
a thumbs up before disintegrating completely,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.820 --> 00:09:02.820
letting John know everything is going to be okay.&nbsp;
Despite that reassurance, poor John is in tears,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.820 --> 00:09:06.600
and so is everybody watching the saddest&nbsp;
ending in Schwarzenegger's career.

00:09:09.060 --> 00:09:17.280
One last Ram Jam
 
With movies like Requiem for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.280 --> 00:09:22.380
a Dream in his filmography, it's safe to say that&nbsp;
Darren Aronofsky loves a truly depressing ending,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.380 --> 00:09:27.840
but of all the films he's ever directed, none&nbsp;
have caused as much sobbing as The Wrestler. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.840 --> 00:09:33.240
gritty and grim drama follows the story of Randy&nbsp;
"The Ram" Robinson, a professional wrestler who's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.240 --> 00:09:38.760
far from his in-ring glory days. This dude has&nbsp;
a lived a hard life and made some bad decisions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.760 --> 00:09:42.780
and now he's all alone. His daughter&nbsp;
doesn't want anything to do with him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.780 --> 00:09:47.940
his romantic life isn't working out, and the world&nbsp;
outside the ring is nothing but pain and misery.

00:09:47.940 --> 00:09:51.540
Lonely and depressed, Randy gets&nbsp;
back into the wrestling game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.540 --> 00:09:56.040
where at least the fans respect and adore&nbsp;
him. But stepping back into the ring comes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.040 --> 00:10:02.220
at a high price. Randy has a bad heart and knows&nbsp;
that another match might be his last. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.220 --> 00:10:06.540
when he hears the cheers of the crowd, the&nbsp;
wrestler climbs up on the turnbuckle to perform&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.540 --> 00:10:12.000
his signature move…even as his heart starts to&nbsp;
fail him. When he leaps down onto his opponent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:15.660
we know it's the last time he'll&nbsp;
ever do the legendary "Ram Jam,”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.660 --> 00:10:19.740
but it's all worth for it Randy, because&nbsp;
he's living and dying for the only family&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.740 --> 00:10:25.418
he's ever known, the fans… who now have to watch&nbsp;
their hero die in the ring in front of them.

00:10:25.418 --> 00:10:30.240
Man on Fire may not have been a favorite of&nbsp;
critics, but Denzel Washington's performance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.240 --> 00:10:36.540
as alcoholic bodyguard John Creasy was one of the&nbsp;
most moving of his career. After years of booze&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.540 --> 00:10:42.300
and bullets, Creasy gets a shot at redemption as&nbsp;
a bodyguard for Pita, played by Dakota Fanning.

00:10:42.300 --> 00:10:48.240
When a powerful crime syndicate captures Pita,&nbsp;
Creasy buys pretty much every gun in Mexico and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.240 --> 00:10:51.660
goes on a murder spree, eventually&nbsp;
capturing the kidnapper's brother.

00:10:51.660 --> 00:10:57.660
Dying from a gunshot wound, Creasy trades&nbsp;
himself for the safety of his charge. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.660 --> 00:11:02.940
before handing himself over, he reunites&nbsp;
with Pita for just a few seconds. When he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.940 --> 00:11:07.760
tells her goodbye, it's a moment that will&nbsp;
make even the toughest action fan weep.

00:11:07.760 --> 00:11:11.035
"Where are you going?"

00:11:13.811 --> 00:11:16.139
"I'm goin' home, too."

00:11:16.139 --> 00:11:18.120
While the boxing drama Million Dollar 

00:11:18.120 --> 00:11:25.916
Baby may at times feel as inspirational as Rocky,&nbsp;
Rocky Balboa never ended a movie quite like this.

00:11:25.916 --> 00:11:31.500
Hilary Swank plays Maggie Fitzgerald, a backwoods&nbsp;
waitress hoping to make her name as a boxer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.500 --> 00:11:35.100
and director Clint Eastwood plays&nbsp;
grizzled trainer Frankie Dunn.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.100 --> 00:11:39.960
Dunn is reluctant to teach a woman at&nbsp;
first, especially one as old as Maggie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.960 --> 00:11:44.280
but eventually her persistence wins&nbsp;
him over. Maggie proves her worth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.280 --> 00:11:50.160
KOing her way to a championship fight — and&nbsp;
that's when the movie takes a hard left turn.

00:11:50.160 --> 00:11:55.920
Maggie is paralyzed from the waist down when her&nbsp;
opponent illegally punches her after the bell.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.920 --> 00:12:01.920
Our hero falls hard from the punch, smashing her&nbsp;
neck against a footstool. Maggie loses her leg,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.920 --> 00:12:06.480
and she begs her trainer to end her&nbsp;
suffering. Dunn refuses at first,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.480 --> 00:12:13.140
but agrees after Maggie tries and fails to&nbsp;
commit suicide. Before Dunn administers the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.140 --> 00:12:18.282
lethal injection, he tells Maggie&nbsp;
the meaning of her Irish nickname.

00:12:18.982 --> 00:12:26.282
'Mo Cuishle' means 'my darling, my blood.'

00:12:26.282 --> 00:12:30.780
Even steely-eyed Eastwood can't keep&nbsp;
the tears back here, and you know if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.780 --> 00:12:36.000
even Dirty Harry is getting weepy, there's no&nbsp;
way you're going to be able to hold it back.

00:12:36.000 --> 00:12:42.946
"Niagara Falls, Frankie Angel."

00:12:42.946 --> 00:12:44.160
After the tragic death&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.160 --> 00:12:50.820
of conservationist Timothy Treadwell at the jaws&nbsp;
of a brown bear, director Werner Herzog assembled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.820 --> 00:12:56.400
Treadwell's footage and created Grizzly Man --&nbsp;
one of the most moving documentaries of all time.

00:12:56.400 --> 00:13:02.160
Grizzly Man follows Treadwell as he journeys into&nbsp;
the Alaskan wilderness, interacts with animals,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.160 --> 00:13:07.740
and shares his unique view about man's place in&nbsp;
nature. While Treadwell's behavior is sometimes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.740 --> 00:13:12.600
questionable, it's hard not to admire his&nbsp;
passion. With the help of Herzog's guiding hand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.600 --> 00:13:17.520
we grow attached to Treadwell as Grizzly&nbsp;
Man heads for its inevitable tragic ending.

00:13:17.520 --> 00:13:22.860
While we go into Grizzly Man knowing Treadwell's&nbsp;
fate, it's upsetting to finally say goodbye to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.860 --> 00:13:28.260
such a unique soul. In the film's final moments,&nbsp;
we watch for the last time as Treadwell wanders&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:28.260 --> 00:13:33.000
into the woods, accompanied by two fuzzy&nbsp;
friends and a mournful country song that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:33.000 --> 00:13:35.238
feels like it was written just for him.

00:13:35.238 --> 00:13:37.164
While Wes Anderson directs comedies, 

00:13:37.164 --> 00:13:42.180
there's always a deep sense of melancholy&nbsp;
underlying his work. That's especially true&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:42.180 --> 00:13:48.600
for The Grand Budapest Hotel. Set in the fictional&nbsp;
European country of Zubrowka, the film's narrative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:48.600 --> 00:13:54.840
is nestled inside multiple timelines, with&nbsp;
the bulk of it set during the 1930s. Our hero&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:54.840 --> 00:14:01.380
Zero Moustafa played by both Tony Revolori and F.&nbsp;
Murray Abraham, works as a lobby boy at the Grand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:01.380 --> 00:14:08.460
Budapest Hotel under the guidance of flamboyant&nbsp;
concierge M. Gustave, played by Ralph Fiennes.

00:14:08.460 --> 00:14:13.800
The plot involves a dead heiress, a missing&nbsp;
painting, and a family of greedy goons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:13.800 --> 00:14:20.040
but the drama revolves around the dark shadow&nbsp;
hanging over this dreamworld. In the 1930s, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:20.040 --> 00:14:26.700
hotel is an impossibly charming world of pastel&nbsp;
colors. As the story continues, both Zubrowka&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:26.700 --> 00:14:33.540
and the Grand Budapest are overrun by enemy forces&nbsp;
— quasi-Nazis followed by quasi-Soviets. As the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:33.540 --> 00:14:39.930
totalitarian horrors of the 20th century creep in,&nbsp;
M. Gustave's civilized world starts to fade away.

00:14:39.930 --> 00:14:45.660
Guests stop coming and eventually the building is&nbsp;
torn down. M. Gustave is killed during the war,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:45.660 --> 00:14:51.960
communism washes over Zubrowka, and Zero is left&nbsp;
alone with nothing but his memories of better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:51.960 --> 00:14:56.340
days. The film ends with the nostalgic&nbsp;
author saying over the closing frame,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:56.340 --> 00:15:00.300
"It was an enchanting old ruin, but&nbsp;
I never managed to see it again."
 &nbsp;

00:15:00.300 --> 00:15:05.580
On its cold, gray surface, The Blackcoat's&nbsp;
Daughter is a movie about a girl who's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:05.580 --> 00:15:10.080
demonically possessed. But under the&nbsp;
slow-building dread and eerie music,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:10.080 --> 00:15:15.660
there's a tragic story about a helpless child&nbsp;
afraid of being alone. Played at first by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:15.660 --> 00:15:21.000
Kiernan Shipka, and later by Emma Roberts, Kat&nbsp;
is a boarding school student with some serious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:21.000 --> 00:15:26.040
abandonment issues. When her parents are late to&nbsp;
pick her up for the semester break, Kat freaks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:26.040 --> 00:15:31.200
out. It doesn't help matters any when she has an&nbsp;
unsettling dream about her parents' untimely fate.

00:15:31.200 --> 00:15:36.420
Terrified her parents are dead and that she'll&nbsp;
be all alone in the world, Kat literally makes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:36.420 --> 00:15:41.520
a deal with the devil, allowing herself to be&nbsp;
possessed in exchange for companionship. She's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:41.520 --> 00:15:46.440
so afraid of losing the spirit's company,&nbsp;
she beheads three people at its urging.

00:15:46.440 --> 00:15:52.020
When a priest exorcises the demon from Kat,&nbsp;
she's so scared of being alone that she begs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:52.020 --> 00:15:57.480
the beast to stay. Unfortunately, her pleas&nbsp;
are no match for holy water. Years later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:57.480 --> 00:16:03.240
an older Kat murders two new victims and brings&nbsp;
their heads back to the school, hoping her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:03.240 --> 00:16:08.520
sacrifice will summon the demon. When she realizes&nbsp;
the evil spirit has left the school behind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:08.520 --> 00:16:14.280
the movie ends with Kat sobbing and screaming&nbsp;
into the wintry void. She realizes now that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:14.280 --> 00:16:20.820
she's truly by herself. Nobody will ever come to&nbsp;
pick her up, and even the devil doesn't want her.

00:16:20.820 --> 00:16:25.740
Directed by Damien Chazelle, La La&nbsp;
Land follows two star-crossed lovers:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:25.740 --> 00:16:31.860
Emma Stone as aspiring actress Mia and jazz&nbsp;
devotee Sebastian played by Ryan Gosling.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:31.860 --> 00:16:37.140
Mia and Sebastian enjoy the happiness and&nbsp;
heartache all long-term relationships face.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:37.140 --> 00:16:41.700
Eventually, because of their conflicting&nbsp;
ambitions, they go their separate ways.

00:16:41.700 --> 00:16:47.880
Five years later, Mia is a movie star and&nbsp;
Sebastian runs a thriving nightclub. One&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:47.880 --> 00:16:52.980
night they lock eyes across a room, and we're&nbsp;
treated to an elaborate dream sequence. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:52.980 --> 00:16:58.380
the kind of ending we expect to see — the guy and&nbsp;
girl end up with one another and live happily ever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:58.380 --> 00:17:05.222
after — but the dream soon comes to an end with&nbsp;
Mia and Sebastian once again going their own ways.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:05.222 --> 00:17:11.100
It's a heartbreaking reminder that the choices&nbsp;
we make always leave the door open for regret.

00:17:11.100 --> 00:17:16.680
Before Tony Stark made his Iron Man suit and&nbsp;
before Christian Bale donned the Bat-cowl,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:16.680 --> 00:17:23.100
there was Hugh Jackman as Wolverine. Jackman&nbsp;
played Logan to perfection, to the point where it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:23.100 --> 00:17:28.740
seems almost impossible that any actor could ever&nbsp;
replace him in the role. He defined Wolverine for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:28.740 --> 00:17:36.060
well over a decade, appearing in every X-Men movie&nbsp;
up until his blood-soaked swan song, 2017's Logan.

00:17:36.060 --> 00:17:41.400
Set in the near-distant future, Logan&nbsp;
follows a dying Wolverine as he uses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:41.400 --> 00:17:45.480
the last of his strength to protect&nbsp;
the young Laura who Logan eventually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:45.480 --> 00:17:51.540
learns is actually his daughter. A shady&nbsp;
scientist wants Laura for nefarious purposes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:51.540 --> 00:17:57.360
and he has the vicious cybernetic Reavers&nbsp;
under his command. At first Logan is such&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:57.360 --> 00:18:03.180
a broken man that he wants nothing to do with&nbsp;
Laura, and only the aging Professor X convinces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:03.180 --> 00:18:07.860
him to do the right thing. Unfortunately&nbsp;
Professor X doesn't survive the journey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:07.860 --> 00:18:12.780
but Logan continues to help Laura all the&nbsp;
same. When Laura and other mutant children&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:12.780 --> 00:18:18.851
try to escape into Canada, Wolverine makes his&nbsp;
final stand in the woods to protect their escape.

00:18:18.851 --> 00:18:24.660
But even after fighting off all the Reavers, Logan&nbsp;
still must face a clone made to be a stronger,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:24.660 --> 00:18:28.200
younger version of himself. With the&nbsp;
help of Laura and her young allies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:28.200 --> 00:18:32.460
the clone is killed, but Logan's wounds&nbsp;
are too much for him. Thankfully,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:32.460 --> 00:18:38.160
after a brutal life full of hard decisions,&nbsp;
Logan enjoys a moment of fatherly love before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:38.160 --> 00:18:43.860
shuffling off this mortal coil. In the film's&nbsp;
final scene, a tearful Laura gives Logan the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:43.860 --> 00:18:49.380
eulogy he deserves, quoting Alan Ladd's final&nbsp;
monologue from the classic western Shane.

00:18:49.380 --> 00:18:57.849
"There's no living with the killing. There’s no going back. 
Right or wrong, it’s a brand. A brand that sticks." 

00:18:57.849 --> 00:19:02.160
After ending her speech, Laura tips the&nbsp;
cross on his grave over on its side,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:02.160 --> 00:19:05.880
marking the Wolverine's final&nbsp;
resting place with an X.

00:19:05.880 --> 00:19:10.080
While the past few years have given&nbsp;
us plenty of amazing superhero movies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:10.080 --> 00:19:16.986
Logan was the first superhero film to truly make&nbsp;
audiences weep for such an iconic character.

00:19:16.986 --> 00:19:23.100
Since Thanos' first MCU appearance in 2012,&nbsp;
Marvel fans couldn't wait for the big purple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:23.100 --> 00:19:28.140
baddie to throw down with the Avengers.&nbsp;
The MCU filmmakers only gave us brief,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:28.140 --> 00:19:33.120
teasing glimpses of the Mad Titan; first a&nbsp;
little bit in the initial Avengers movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:33.120 --> 00:19:39.240
then a lot more in Guardians of the Galaxy,&nbsp;
and later in Avengers: Age of Ultron. Finally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:39.240 --> 00:19:46.080
six years after Avengers, Thanos finally arrived&nbsp;
with his Infinity Gauntlet, and Earth's mightiest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:46.080 --> 00:19:51.540
heroes were completely overmatched. It looked&nbsp;
like Thor was going to save the day when he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:51.540 --> 00:19:57.191
slammed Stormbreaker into the Mad Titan's chest,&nbsp;
but as the God of Thunder quickly learned —

00:19:57.191 --> 00:20:02.111
"You should've gone for the head." End on the snap

00:20:02.640 --> 00:20:09.120
And just like that, with a snap of his fingers,&nbsp;
Thanos wiped out half the population of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:09.120 --> 00:20:14.640
universe, including the majority of our beloved&nbsp;
heroes. Along with most of the Avengers turning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:14.640 --> 00:20:20.760
to dust, almost all the Guardians fell victim&nbsp;
to Thanos' purge. Rocket was there to watch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:20.760 --> 00:20:26.820
Teenage Groot fade away, and to hear his final&nbsp;
"I Am Groot." Adding to the sorrow of many fans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:26.820 --> 00:20:30.720
Guardians of the Galaxy's writer and&nbsp;
director James Gunn would eventually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:30.720 --> 00:20:36.000
reveal that Groot's last "I am Groot"&nbsp;
translated into the word, "Dad."

00:20:36.000 --> 00:20:40.800
For many fans, Peter Parker's death was&nbsp;
the most painful to watch. Even though&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:40.800 --> 00:20:46.926
he just fought the toughest villain in the MCU,&nbsp;
Peter's still just a kid, afraid of fading away.

00:20:46.926 --> 00:20:52.800
Infinity War might have the darkest ending of any&nbsp;
major blockbuster ever. There's no moral victory;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:52.800 --> 00:20:59.100
no feel good speech. For the first time, the&nbsp;
Avengers have truly lost, and it all ends&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:59.100 --> 00:21:06.000
with Thanos admiring his handiwork, smiling&nbsp;
as the sun rises on a "grateful universe.

